03:30.680 --> 03:35.260
The median average workout has $955 saved for retirement, okay?

03:35.320 --> 03:38.580
Not 955k, 955 bucks.

03:38.580 --> 03:41.480
When it comes to this conversation, people tend to quote the average.

03:41.760 --> 03:46.440
But a handful of rich savers drags that average up and hides the fact the guy in the middle basically has nothing.

03:46.640 --> 03:48.180
And here's what drives Rao mad.

03:48.320 --> 03:50.120
Everyone calls this a discipline problem.

03:50.460 --> 03:51.340
Should have saved more.

03:51.500 --> 03:53.180
But you can't save your way out of this.

03:53.320 --> 03:56.740
The whole plan was earn a wage, buy assets, hold for 40 years.

03:57.040 --> 03:59.600
This worked perfectly when wages kept up with assets.

03:59.720 --> 04:00.520
But they don't anymore.

04:00.720 --> 04:04.440
Wages have lost half their purchase in power versus the S&P since 2008.

04:04.440 --> 04:08.900
So you're told to buy the one thing that runs away from your paycheck faster every year.

04:09.040 --> 04:11.520
So even the people that did everything right are still screwed.

04:11.760 --> 04:13.140
It's not them, it's the plan.

04:13.360 --> 04:17.460
So massive, massive difference in the average versus the median.

04:17.660 --> 04:21.020
And it does seem more and more like all of this kind of fits together, right?

04:21.060 --> 04:25.720
You've got your exit plans in the forms of hard assets, gold, Bitcoin, et al.

04:25.720 --> 04:29.000
